description:
Destination: 'backup' and 'restore' conform to appspec/06-sync-operations.md as ONE procedure parameterized by the direction record (appspec/06 gives the exact table) — per-file procedure, the backup-only link-skip, drift integration with the stdout stream note, the replace prompts with their exact nouns. Because these are the first commands that sync, this ticket also lands everything appspec/01 defines system-wide that they need, built once for all five commands:
- the two-level sorted fan-out and single-app scoping — named app replaces scope and overrides both config lists, validated before the environment gate (appspec/01 §1 §3, appspec/02 'Selecting which applications');
- the Mackup-folder ensure/require gates with the folder-creation prompt and its decline error (appspec/06 'Environment gate per command', appspec/01 §4 levels 2-3);
- the ONE confirmation mechanism — three-valued force/force-no/interactive policy threaded as data to every prompt, y/yes/n/no case-insensitive, re-ask on anything else, EOF at a prompt is a nonzero failure (appspec/07 'The confirmation / safety model', appspec/01 §3);
- dry-run and verbose semantics per appspec/01 §3, including the one dry-run exception (the folder-creation gate is not a per-file mutation and still runs) and verbose observational purity;
- the partial-failure contract: per-file copy failures collected as data, stderr 'Error: Unable to copy' lines, end-of-run incomplete summary, exit 1 (appspec/06 'Partial-failure contract', appspec/01 §5).

Spec constraints, verbatim intent: any divergence between backup and restore beyond {direction, user-facing wording, the one link-skip} is a defect (appspec/01 §1). The link commands must reuse this fan-out, gating, and confirmation — five leaves on one tree (appspec/01 §1).

Why: this is Strategy A of appspec/00 — half the product — and it establishes the shared machinery the link epic plugs into.

Done-claim: a second identical backup run does nothing and prompts for nothing; a run with an uncopyable file prints the per-file stderr error, the end summary, and exits 1; 'backup vim' acts on vim while vim sits in applications_to_ignore; 'backup <unknown>' fails with 'Unsupported application:' before any folder is created or prompt shown; --dry-run mutates no config file on any path.
